+# Surrogate modelling examples: BESOS/harlow -> bdopt
+
+Each `Test N` folder now has a `bdopt_Example_N.ipynb` next to the original `Besos_Example_N.ipynb` and
+`Harlow_Example_N.ipynb` (kept for reference). The new notebooks use the
+[`bdopt`](https://github.com/lorenzobottaccioli/buidlingdesginoptimization) library:
+
+* same models, weather files, design parameters and objectives as the originals;
+* adaptive sampling (`AdaptiveSampler`) instead of the hand-written harlow glue: no copied bounds, no scalers,
+  no `target_f`, no `update` patch;
+* three surrogate models compared on the same held-out simulations (Gaussian process, scikit-learn MLP,
+  PyTorch MLP) instead of TensorFlow/Keras;
+* NSGA-II (pymoo) on the best surrogate, and the selected design checked with a real EnergyPlus run.
+
+Edit the budget cell (`N_INITIAL`, `N_TEST`, `MAX_ITER`, `TOL`, `PROCESSES`) at the top; simulations are cached in
+`outputdir/`, so re-running or resuming never repeats a simulation.
+
+Differences from the originals:
+
+* **Test 6 (windows):** the original created one design variable per window (the lab building has 9 windows) all
+  named `Windows types`, and the harlow notebook's bounds were written for 7 columns. The new notebook uses one
+  variable, "Windows types", applied to every window.
+* Objectives are in Joules, as before.
+* The `epw` package weather summary was dropped (not needed).
+
+The notebooks were generated with `tools/besos_to_bdopt_notebook.py` (bdopt repository) and executed end to end
+with a reduced budget against EnergyPlus 9.6 to check them.
